+# Editorial record: final paper, Comment, and briefing
+
+Samuel Mausberg · 2 October 2026
+
+## Baseline and preservation
+
+The baseline is the supplied `memory_return_publication_bundle.zip`, containing the 16-page article and 2-page Comment. This pass produces an 18-page article, a 1-page Comment, and a new 3-page discussion briefing. The statement-only packet remains 10 pages and the cover email remains 1 page.
+
+All 15 numbered statement source files are byte-identical to the baseline. The ten source files containing the full proofs, output-only results, finite-memory result, and version-specific audit are also byte-identical. The verification appendix has an added description of the new computations and an editorial-history correction. The main text's existing displayed mathematical formulas are preserved. The prose and Comment have been edited without changing the mathematical conclusions or their hypotheses.
+
+`verification/editorial_preservation.json` records the statement and appendix hashes. `verification/baseline_hashes.json` preserves the baseline hashes for a portable rerun. `verification/source_changes.diff` records every source-line change. `verification/changed_files.json` lists every added, changed, or removed source, code, data, and support file. These checks establish editorial preservation; they do not establish correctness of the proofs.
+
+No new mathematical error was identified during this editorial pass. `MATHEMATICAL_FLAGS.md` records that status and separates three plotting distinctions from corrections. The preceding revision's orthogonal-pure-state endpoint qualification remains documented in `EDITORIAL_CHANGES.md`; it was not introduced in the present pass.
+
+## Labels and cross-references
+
+The common theorem counter previously caused cleveref to call every numbered statement a theorem. The shared sequence is retained, with distinct alias counters for lemmas, propositions, and corollaries. Their references now preserve the correct type. In particular, the appendix headings read “Proof of Corollary 2,” “Proof of Proposition 5,” and “Proof of Corollary 7.” The two manually typed theorem/lemma references in the main text were checked against their targets. Numbering and statement content are unchanged.
+
+The referee packet was regenerated from the final article labels and recompiled. Its statements and source roles remain the same. The unsent email's two PDF attachments were refreshed and verified by hash; the email prose and recipients were not edited.
+
+## PRL Comment policy and length
+
+The current PRL author page gives Comments and Replies a limit of 750 words. The APS length guide counts body and caption text, single-column displayed mathematics at 16 word-equivalents per row, and a single-column figure at 150 divided by its width-to-height ratio, plus 20. Title, authors, date, bibliography, and acknowledgments are excluded. Both pages were checked on 2 October 2026:
+
+- https://journals.aps.org/prl/authors
+- https://journals.aps.org/authors/length-guide
+
+The new Comment compiles to one page in the ordinary PRL REVTeX reprint format. Its four displayed rows contribute 64 word-equivalents. The compact figure contributes about 76. A token estimate including body, caption, inline formulas, and data availability gives about 530 word-equivalents. A deliberately more generous variant charges every inline TeX command or alphanumeric token separately and gives about 688. `code/editorial_audit.py` reproduces both estimates and `verification/comment_length.json` records their components. These are author estimates; the journal determines its production count.
+
+The policy also requires timeliness. This pass does not resolve how the editors would apply that criterion to a Comment on the 2022 Letter.
+
+## Changes to the Comment
+
+The introduction is reduced to the two issues under examination. The version audit retains the v1 supremum and displayed limiting conventions, the published infimum and fixed first-use numerator, the supplement's restricted initializer and geometric-deterioration approximation, the later current-error joint limit, and the thesis's reservoir-first order. Locators remain beside those definitions.
+
+The model and row–column identity are retained, followed by the finite-block relaxation argument, fidelity floor, exact single-parameter limits, and the two iterated limits. The product counterexample retains both coupling ranges, including the second-cell adjustment for q below one third. It also retains the orthogonality and relaxation step that forces the capability infimum to zero.
+
+The extended discussion, duplicative transitions, long bibliographic formatting, and references outside the three requested subjects have been removed from the Comment. Those materials remain in the article's unchanged source-audit appendix. The Comment has no abstract or appendix. It retains its AI-assistance acknowledgment and data-availability statement.
+
+A compact certified ratio figure is added. It is anchored as a column-width minipage, with a normal numbered caption, so that balancing the one-page REVTeX layout cannot interrupt a sentence or split an inline formula around a float. No font-size, margin, or class-file compression was used to meet the limit.
+
+## Main-text prose and organization
+
+A compact notation table follows the definitions. It identifies horizon, memory dimension, homogenizer size, the two tolerances, the output and memory states, and the partial-swap parameters.
+
+Section III now separates entropy balance, its exact-return consequence, approximate return, the pure-initializer bound, independent randomization, and the dyadic purifier into shorter paragraphs. Each main-text numbered result is followed by a physical explanation before its appendix pointer. The explanations concern entropy capacity, Pauli-label rank, spectral dilation, accumulated reservoir disturbance, excitation transport, collective spin relaxation, or singlet population, according to the result.
+
+Section V is shortened to five connected paragraphs: constructor attributes and continued performance; the invariant marginal-capability set; what state return controls for an independently supplied future experiment; the two deterioration definitions; and the order of the device and horizon quantifiers. The invariant-set formula is unchanged. References to the complete audit and proofs remain in place.
+
+The scope section now distinguishes certified plotting intervals from exact algebraic checks and floating-point tests. The data-availability statement mentions the figure data. The abstract, author identity, assistance acknowledgment, resource tables, and mathematical statements are retained. All earlier limitations remain collected in the scope section, with the detailed computational status also documented in the verification record.
+
+## Existing diagrams
+
+All three editable TikZ diagrams are retained and redrawn to fit one column with readable labels. They use black strokes and restrained gray fills, without reliance on hue.
+
+The fair-bit diagram now shows the memory and three separate input wires, each CNOT, and the two possible joint output strings. The grid diagram retains its row and column orderings with consistent input and reservoir labels and larger cell numbers. The cyclic-register diagram identifies the leading pure bits, dyadic tail, appended mixed inputs, and the total counted memory. Captions describe the physical initialization, operation, and observation without requiring the surrounding text.
+
+## Quantitative figures and computation
+
+Six standalone vector plots are added, assembled into three article figures and one compact Comment figure. The article's memory-cost and ratio figures each contain two separately generated column-width plots. The singlet plot occupies one column. Every series is distinguished by line style, marker, or grayscale band. Captions state the model parameters, quantities, and calculation method.
+
+The ratio scan uses eta = pi/4. At fixed two uses, it evaluates reservoir sizes 1 through 32. The forward band is the rigorous enclosure e <= R <= 4e from the reservoir-fidelity floor; forward markers through M = 6 additionally calculate reservoir fidelity using exact characteristic polynomials and rational root isolation. The reverse curve uses an exact conditional column channel. At fixed M = 2, both directions are evaluated through 48 uses using exact rational memory updates and rational square-root enclosures. Certified ratio interval widths are below 1e-28; the largest observed width is about 5.40e-30. The fixed-size limit is 2, consistent with the theorem.
+
+The singlet plot uses k = 1,...,32, M = k^5, and eta = 2 arctan[1/(2 k^2)]. Sine and cosine are rational. The exact 16-by-16 Pauli transfer matrix is powered with 512-bit fixed-point enclosures, with every rounding step included in the radius. Axial symmetry reduces the spectral calculation to scalar blocks and one 2-by-2 block. The largest trace-error interval width is below 5e-114. The plot also shows the exact singlet lower bound and the rigorous prefix-return upper bound. Coupling varies with reservoir size as required for this small-return limit.
+
+The memory-cost plots evaluate the exact integer randomization optimum and the spectral lower/dyadic upper purification bounds. They scan n = 1,...,128 at delta = 0.1, and delta = 1/t for t = 4,...,100 at n = 32. The gray interval bounds the optimum; its edges are not measured performance or a claim of exact finite-size equality.
+
+`code/certified_figures.py` generates the rational data and certificates. `code/make_plots.py` generates the vector figures and inspection PNGs. Every rational interval endpoint is retained in `data/*.json`; the CSV files contain decimal plotting coordinates. The new computations use no reservoir factorization, sampling, or floating-point eigensolver. Twenty-four exact row–column comparisons and four interval-power comparisons passed. Asymptotic conclusions continue to rely on the unchanged analytic proofs.
+
+The new verification subsection explains the interval arithmetic and its error bound. This is a calculation certificate for the requested figures, separate from the paper's existing theorem statements. The historical verification record is retained rather than represented as a new independent review.
+
+## Briefing
+
+The new three-page briefing gives one page to each main result. It explains entropy balance and the memory-cost comparison; weak-collision return and the singlet obstruction; and the version-specific limit and capability issues. It addresses dimension-dependent approximate catalysis, mixed initialization, correlations, joint scaling, endpoint versus prefix return, and restrictions on the capability set. Section and appendix pointers support a discussion by email. It also states the unresolved many-use optimum and the review status.
+
+## Build and visual inspection
+
+All five document PDFs were recompiled. The three proof-heading types were checked in extracted PDF text. The final logs contain no unresolved references, multiply defined labels, overfull boxes, or stuck-float warnings. The article has benign underfull-line notices and the known nameref/label-definition notice; neither corresponds to clipping or missing content in the inspected pages.
+
+All 33 document pages were rendered and visually inspected: article 18, Comment 1, briefing 3, referee packet 10, and email 1. The new plot panels were also inspected at their intended column width. Edits during inspection corrected a clipped terminal tick, improved a compact-plot annotation, moved a cost annotation clear of its curve, prevented the Comment's figure from splitting an inline expression, and anchored the singlet figure next to its discussion. The existing diagrams and new captions remain legible in grayscale. Render records and page images are retained under `verification/renders/editorial/`.
+
+The build script now handles the Comment's manual bibliography, compiles the briefing, refreshes the packet, and repackages the unsent email. Requirements and reproduction instructions were updated for the plotting code. No email was sent, no mailbox draft was created, and no manuscript was submitted or posted.
